From 259e1cb30fa2cdfa1dcaac701c9f45f89da2c270 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jeffrey Frederick <5095066+jeffreyfrederick@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 21:37:29 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] Repaint disjoint damage rects separately instead of their
 union

The draw system unioned the bounds of every view that needed a redraw
into a single dirty rect and repainted every view intersecting it. Two
small animations at opposite ends of a window (e.g. a playhead in the
middle and a level meter in a corner, or a clock in a title bar) made
the whole window repaint every frame, including all static stylesheet
views (backgrounds, shadows, text) in between.

Track damage as a small set of rects (`DamageRegion`): rects are snapped
to whole device pixels, overlapping/touching rects are merged, and the
count is capped (6) by merging the pair whose union wastes the least
area. Each rect is cleared and repainted in its own pass under a plain
rect clip (not a region clip, which can push the GPU backend onto
software clip masks); views outside a pass's rect are skipped as before.
No damage still means a full repaint, and filtered views keep their
"repaint when anything they overlap is damaged" rule.

Tested on the ChipDAW GUI prototype during playback (damage: playhead
area, meters, a text field in the top bar, a column of small badges),
Windows/NVIDIA, 60 Hz: process CPU 16.3% -> 10.3% (43-clip demo),
16.4% -> 11.2% (200 clips). Static-frame pixel diff: no content
differences; dynamic captures (dragging, dial value bubble, meters,
playhead) show no stale pixels or seams.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
